<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the generated bundle Info.plist by tauri-bundler (any keys
  here are added to the ones tauri.conf.json already produces).

  Without NSMicrophoneUsageDescription, WKWebView does not expose
  `navigator.mediaDevices` to the page at all on this build's WebKit
  (it is `undefined`, not merely permission-denied). That takes out
  headphone cue output enumeration/selection (CUEOUT-09), the audio
  input picker (CUEOUT-10) and the first-select cue latency calibration
  (CUEOUT-11) together, since all three start from
  `navigator.mediaDevices`. Confirmed live on silver Mon 14 Sep 2026:
  every attempt logged
  "headphone output unsupported: navigator.mediaDevices is unavailable"
  in webui-client-errors-2026-09-14.log, and codesign's entitlements dump
  (`codesign -d` with its entitlements option) on the installed app returned
  nothing at all. No double hyphen in this comment: XML forbids it there.

  An input is read for three things: enumerating output devices
  (enumerateDevices requires a granted input permission before device
  labels are populated), the short calibration click-train capture, and
  set recording (SET-10), which records the input the DJ picks on the REC
  button to the set's folder on this Mac. Nothing is sent anywhere. The
  hardened runtime also needs the audio-input entitlement
  (Entitlements.app.plist) before any of these can hear anything.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Open DJ lists your audio devices, briefly listens to auto-calibrate headphone cue latency, and records your set from the input you pick when you press Record. Audio stays on this Mac and is never sent anywhere.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
